{
    "title": "Fisheries of the Northeastern United States; Summer Flounder Fishery; Quota Transfer From North Carolina to Massachusetts",
    "type": "Rule",
    "date": "December 04, 2025",
    "url": "https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/FR-2025-12-04/pdf/2025-21960.pdf",
    "summary": "Two quick federal updates: the health department removed some expired foreign quarantine rules, and fisheries managers approved small, end-of-year catch limit transfers between a few East Coast states.\n\nOn public health, the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention formally took out two temporary parts of its foreign quarantine regulation that had already expired on November 21, 2025. The core rules that were finalized in 2017 stay in place. This housekeeping step clears up the rulebook so ports, carriers, and travelers know exactly what standards apply going forward. People who favor streamlined, predictable rules will welcome the clarity. Others may worry that taking expired provisions off the books could signal fewer tools at hand if a new health threat arises, though the baseline quarantine authorities remain.\n\nOn fisheries, the National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration approved two short-term quota swaps to close out 2025. North Carolina shifted a small amount of its summer flounder allocation to Massachusetts to square up landings made under a safe harbor situation earlier in the year. This helps ensure states\u2019 catch tallies are fair and accurate and that annual limits are still met. That said, Massachusetts had already hit its federal flounder limit in November, so this bookkeeping move doesn\u2019t reopen federal landings there for the rest of the year. It mainly benefits accountability between states, while slightly reducing North Carolina\u2019s remaining flounder share.\n\nIn a separate action, Rhode Island transferred a portion of its commercial bluefish allocation to North Carolina. This gives North Carolina vessels a bit more room to operate at year\u2019s end, while Rhode Island gives up a small late-season amount it did not expect to use. Fishermen and dealers in the receiving state may see modest, immediate benefits; those in the sending state could face tighter late-season flexibility, though the transfers are designed to address real-time needs without exceeding overall conservation limits.\n\nOverall, these are targeted, practical adjustments: one to keep the health rules current, and two to keep shared fisheries fair and sustainable as the year wraps up.",
